<article class="page">
<header>
<div class="eyebrow">Startup assessment</div>
<h1>OnDemandApps.ai</h1>
<p>Markets, private workflows, and the economics of a focused launch.</p>
<div class="meta"><span>September 20, 2026 · Strategy analysis</span><button type="button" onclick="window.print()">Print / Save as PDF</button></div>
</header>
<main>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Report sections"><a href="#assessment">Assessment</a><a href="#markets">Five markets</a><a href="#privacy">Privacy</a><a href="#economics">Customer economics</a><a href="#revenue">Revenue potential</a><a href="#launch">Launch plan</a></nav>
<section id="assessment" aria-label="Assessment">
<p class="lead"><strong>I think there is a viable business here—but I would launch it as a specialized, privately deployed workflow service, then build the broader app platform from repeated customer needs.</strong></p>
<p>Your engineering experience fits the delivery problem well. The harder commercial problem will be finding workflows that are valuable enough to buy, similar enough to reuse, and still poorly served by existing software.</p>
<p>My first two markets would be <strong>industrial distributors</strong> and <strong>specialty manufacturers</strong>. Both offer document-heavy operations, commercially sensitive information, and workflows that cross departments. I would investigate freight forwarding next, and approach insurance and accounting only with a strong industry partner.</p>
<div class="note"><p>The numbers below are <strong>explicit business-model assumptions to validate</strong>, not claimed market prices or forecasts. Sources establish existing workflows and competition; they do not establish willingness to buy your particular service.</p></div>
<h2>What I would change about your concept</h2>
<p>Your proposed sequence—describe an app, try it, pay, install it—is attractive for demonstrations. For business-critical software, it leaves several expensive problems unresolved:</p>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Business model adjustments"><table class="comparison"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Your proposal</th><th scope="col">My concern</th><th scope="col">Recommended adjustment</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Customers post an app request</td><td>Customers often describe screens rather than the underlying operational problem</td><td>Guided discovery around documents, decisions, exceptions, users, and existing systems</td></tr>
<tr><td>Customers select their technology stack</td><td>Every additional stack expands testing, deployment, and maintenance costs</td><td>Support one standard stack initially; sell compatibility with their existing systems</td></tr>
<tr><td>Build before payment</td><td>You absorb discovery and customization costs for prospects who may never buy</td><td>Free synthetic-data demonstration, followed by paid discovery and a scoped pilot</td></tr>
<tr><td>Test on your servers</td><td>Privacy-sensitive customers may object before you reach the installation stage</td><td>Synthetic data on your servers; real-document pilots inside the customer’s environment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Customer follows AI installation instructions</td><td>Successful installation does not establish reliable operations</td><td>Managed installation, upgrades, backups, recovery, and clear support responsibilities</td></tr>
<tr><td>Human programmers join occasionally</td><td>Process clarification and exception handling may require substantial human work</td><td>Price human delivery into the service from the beginning</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>The deliverable customers will pay for is a dependable business process.</strong> Generating the application is one part of delivering that process.</p>
<p>A positioning statement I would test:</p>
<blockquote><p>“We turn your email, PDF, and spreadsheet workflows into private business applications that run in your environment and connect to your existing systems.”</p></blockquote>
</section>
<section id="markets">
<h2>The five markets I would investigate, in order</h2>
<p>These employee ranges are prospecting filters I recommend, not formal industry definitions.</p>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Five target markets"><table class="comparison"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Rank</th><th scope="col">Market and target company</th><th scope="col">First workflow to sell</th><th scope="col">Buyer</th><th scope="col">Principal obstacle</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Industrial distributors, 30–250 employees</td><td>Customer purchase order intake and exception handling</td><td>COO, order-management director, CFO</td><td>Existing ERP automation</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Specialty manufacturers, 50–300 employees</td><td>Supplier-document validation and shipment documentation</td><td>Quality director, operations VP</td><td>Document accuracy and traceability</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Freight forwarders and specialized 3PLs, 30–200 employees</td><td>Shipment-document completeness and billing readiness</td><td>COO, branch manager, finance director</td><td>TMS integration and incumbent features</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Commercial insurance brokers, 30–200 employees</td><td>Submission and renewal packet completeness</td><td>COO, commercial-lines director</td><td>Crowded software ecosystem</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Accounting and outsourced-finance firms, 25–150 employees</td><td>Recurring client-document collection and close exceptions</td><td>Managing partner, CAS practice leader</td><td>Established workflow products and price pressure</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>1. Industrial distributors: my preferred starting point</h3>
<p>Think distributors of industrial fasteners, electrical components, packaging materials, valves, bearings, or specialty chemicals.</p>
<p>A specific first application:</p>
<blockquote><p>Customer email and purchase order → identify customer → map customer part numbers to internal SKUs → validate price, quantity, and delivery requirements → route discrepancies → create an approved ERP order.</p></blockquote>
<p>Related applications could handle supplier confirmations, order changes, returns, and invoice discrepancies.</p>
<p>The valuable work is often in exceptions: an obsolete part number, conflicting quantities between email and attachment, an unusual unit of measure, or a purchase order referencing an expired quote.</p>
<p><strong>Why privacy could matter:</strong> customer-specific pricing, supplier costs, margins, negotiated terms, and customer lists. These are commercial reasons for data control; you do not need to rely on a regulatory pitch.</p>
<p><strong>Why this is difficult:</strong> Epicor already offers sales-order automation covering paper, PDFs, spreadsheets, and email. A generic “PDF-to-order” feature faces direct competition. Your opening is a documented gap in the customer’s installed system, particularly across multiple systems or business-specific approvals. <a href="https://www.epicor.com/en/products/enterprise-resource-planning-erp/kinetic/sales-management/ptw-sales-order-automation/">Epicor sales-order automation</a></p>
<p><strong>How to find customers:</strong> ERP implementation partners, distribution-focused IT providers, and targeted outreach to operations leaders. Qualify for a dedicated order-entry team, substantial non-EDI order volume, and measurable rework.</p>
<p><strong>Price I would test:</strong> $25,000 implementation plus $2,000/month for one bounded workflow and a defined integration.</p>
<h3>2. Specialty manufacturing: strongest combination of document complexity and confidentiality</h3>
<p>Target precision machining, fabrication, industrial equipment, and contract component manufacturers.</p>
<p>A strong first application:</p>
<blockquote><p>Supplier material certificates and inspection reports arrive → associate them with purchase order, part revision, and lot → check required documents and fields → flag missing or conflicting evidence → quality employee approves → assemble the shipment documentation package.</p></blockquote>
<p>Additional workflows include:</p>
<ul><li>RFQ intake and routing to estimating.</li><li>Engineering-change acknowledgment across purchasing and production.</li><li>Nonconformance investigation and corrective-action tracking.</li><li>Customer-specific shipment documentation.</li></ul>
<p>The value comes from knowing <strong>which documents belong to which job, revision, and lot</strong>, and preventing incomplete handoffs.</p>
<p><strong>Why privacy could matter:</strong> drawings, manufacturing specifications, customer designs, supplier information, and bid pricing.</p>
<p><strong>Critical limitation:</strong> missing evidence must remain missing. The system should never invent a certification or silently resolve a technical discrepancy.</p>
<p>There is substantial incumbent competition: ProShop already markets digital inspections, nonconformance tracking, and quality-document workflows. Your opportunity is a specific gap around an existing ERP or quality system, rather than rebuilding either system. <a href="https://proshoperp.com/product/quality-systems-inspection/">ProShop quality systems</a></p>
<p><strong>How to find customers:</strong> manufacturing IT providers, ERP consultants, and quality-management consultants. Look for companies where employees repeatedly assemble customer-specific document packages by hand.</p>
<p><strong>Price I would test:</strong> $35,000 implementation plus $2,500/month.</p>
<p>I would initially favor ordinary commercial manufacturing. Highly controlled defense or other sensitive work adds requirements that deserve a separate delivery offering.</p>
<h3>3. Freight forwarding and specialized logistics: measurable operational value</h3>
<p>Target forwarders and 3PLs with a real documentation and billing team.</p>
<p>A first workflow:</p>
<blockquote><p>Shipment documents arrive → associate them with a shipment → check required documents → flag inconsistencies → request missing information through an approved process → mark the shipment ready for billing.</p></blockquote>
<p>Examples include bills of lading, proof of delivery, packing lists, commercial invoices, and supporting charges.</p>
<p>Adjacent opportunities include carrier-invoice reconciliation and customer-specific delivery-document requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Why privacy could matter:</strong> customer identities, shipment patterns, negotiated rates, and commercial terms. However, I would expect efficiency and billing speed to lead the sale; that is a hypothesis to test.</p>
<p><strong>The competitive concern is significant.</strong> CargoWise already offers document storage, missing-document workflows, and integrated forwarding documentation. Before building anything, establish why its existing functionality cannot solve the prospect’s problem. <a href="https://www.cargowise.com/solutions/cargowise-enterprise/edocs/">CargoWise document workflows</a></p>
<p><strong>How to find customers:</strong> logistics software consultants and operational leaders at forwarders with specialized customer requirements. Verify integration access before quoting.</p>
<p><strong>Price I would test:</strong> $25,000 implementation plus $2,000/month.</p>
<p>Keep the initial scope around document handling and approvals. Automated customs decisions or filings would introduce a different level of responsibility.</p>
<h3>4. Commercial insurance brokers: attractive, but a difficult first market</h3>
<p>A possible application:</p>
<blockquote><p>Applications, loss runs, schedules, and emails arrive → associate them with an account and renewal → compare against a checklist → identify missing or inconsistent information → assign follow-up → produce a packet for account-manager review.</p></blockquote>
<p>The best initial scope would be a specialty commercial segment with unusual documentation requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Why privacy could matter:</strong> client financial information, employee information, claims history, and sensitive business details.</p>
<p><strong>Why I rank it fourth:</strong> existing vendors and specialist startups already address much of this. Applied’s ecosystem lists products automating documents and substantial portions of the policy lifecycle; Ivans supports commercial submission connectivity. <a href="https://www1.appliedsystems.com/en-us/about-us/ecosystem/certified-vendors/">Applied certified vendors</a>, <a href="https://www.ivans.com/">Ivans</a></p>
<p>You would need a precise unresolved workflow and access to the agency’s existing software. Broad promises about “AI for insurance paperwork” will be difficult to differentiate.</p>
<p><strong>How to find customers:</strong> through an insurance operations consultant or experienced broker partner who can identify gaps and open doors.</p>
<p><strong>Price I would test:</strong> $30,000 implementation plus $2,500/month.</p>
<h3>5. Accounting and outsourced-finance firms: good repeatability, substantial competition</h3>
<p>I would focus on firms providing recurring outsourced accounting services to businesses.</p>
<p>A first application:</p>
<blockquote><p>Monthly client documents arrive → associate them with client, entity, and period → compare with the close checklist → detect missing statements or supporting invoices → assign exceptions → route completed work to a reviewer.</p></blockquote>
<p>Other applications could manage audit evidence requests or client-specific invoice approval workflows.</p>
<p><strong>Why privacy could matter:</strong> bank statements, payroll, ownership information, financial performance, and tax records.</p>
<p>The attraction is reuse across many clients. The difficulty is that accounting software vendors already provide document collection and workflow automation. Thomson Reuters, for example, connects collection, preparation, review, and storage across its products. <a href="https://tax.thomsonreuters.com/en/tax-accounting/tax-professionals-workflow">Thomson Reuters workflow solutions</a></p>
<p>Your opening would be an unusual cross-system process that existing products handle poorly.</p>
<p><strong>How to find customers:</strong> outsourced-accounting practice leaders and accounting technology consultants. Look for repeated monthly workflows rather than purely seasonal demand.</p>
<p><strong>Price I would test:</strong> $20,000 implementation plus $1,500/month, with client-entity and volume limits.</p>
</section>
<section id="privacy">
<h2>Privacy can help win deals, but it must be precisely defined</h2>
<p>There are different customer requirements:</p>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Privacy requirements"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Customer requirement</th><th scope="col">What your service must actually provide</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>“Do not train models on our information”</td><td>Appropriate provider contracts, product settings, and data handling</td></tr>
<tr><td>“Do not send documents to an external model provider”</td><td>Document processing and model inference inside an approved customer environment</td></tr>
<tr><td>“Keep everything on our premises”</td><td>On-premises processing, storage, models, and an appropriate update/support process</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Commercial AI services can already offer no-training commitments—for example, Claude Enterprise advertises no model training by default. Therefore, “we do not train on your data” alone is a weak differentiator. <a href="https://claude.com/solutions/enterprise">Claude Enterprise</a></p>
<p><strong>Hosting the application on the customer’s server does not prevent data disclosure if it sends documents to an external model API.</strong></p>
<p>For customers requiring that boundary, OCR, inference, embeddings, logs, backups, and support access all need to respect it. Your development process also matters: real customer documents should not enter a coding agent’s context merely because production inference is local.</p>
<p>Self-hosting is also an existing competitive capability; Retool offers deployment inside customer infrastructure. <a href="https://retool.com/govern-enterprise-apps/self-hosted">Retool self-hosting</a></p>
<p>Your stronger differentiation would be the combination of:</p>
<ul><li>A workflow tailored to the customer’s operations.</li><li>Proven integration with their existing systems.</li><li>A verifiable data boundary.</li><li>Ongoing responsibility for keeping the workflow working.</li></ul>
<p>Also distinguish <strong>customer ownership from customer operation</strong>. A company may want its own cloud account while expecting you to manage the application.</p>
</section>
<section id="economics">
<h2>Revenue potential: start with the customer’s economics</h2>
<p>Consider the distributor offering above. These are illustrative assumptions to measure during discovery:</p>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Customer economics assumptions"><table class="numbers"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Input</th><th scope="col">Assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Orders processed monthly</td><td>3,000</td></tr><tr><td>Net handling time saved per order, after review and exceptions</td><td>6 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly capacity released</td><td>300 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Loaded employee cost</td><td>$45/hour</td></tr><tr><td>Gross monthly capacity value</td><td>$13,500</td></tr><tr><td>Portion convertible into useful capacity, avoided overtime, or hiring avoidance</td><td>60%</td></tr><tr class="total"><td>Realizable monthly value</td><td>$8,100</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The $45 assumption is broadly plausible, but must be replaced with the prospect’s actual costs. For context, BLS reported average private-industry compensation of $46.89/hour in June 2026; this is not a role-specific distributor benchmark. <a href="https://www.bls.gov/charts/employer-costs-for-employee-compensation/costs-per-hour.htm">BLS compensation data</a></p>
<p>At $25,000 implementation, $2,000/month support/software, and an <strong>illustrative</strong> $500/month infrastructure cost:</p>
<ul><li>Annual realizable value: <strong>$97,200</strong>.</li><li>First full year of operation’s cost: <strong>$55,000</strong>.</li><li>First-year net value: <strong>$42,200</strong>.</li><li>Implementation payback after go-live: approximately <strong>4.5 months</strong>, assuming benefits begin immediately.</li></ul>
<p>Infrastructure needs benchmarking, especially with local models.</p>
<p>Now consider a smaller prospect: 1,000 orders/month and three minutes saved per order. At the same labor cost and realization factor, monthly value is only <strong>$1,350</strong>—less than the recurring software and infrastructure expense.</p>
<p><strong>That prospect should not buy this package. Volume and actual time savings matter more than employee count.</strong> Capacity released also does not automatically become cash savings.</p>
</section>
<section id="revenue">
<h2>What your own business could earn</h2>
<p>Use a standardized package of <strong>$25,000 implementation + $24,000 annual recurring fees</strong>.</p>
<p>Here is an internally consistent operating scenario, not a forecast:</p>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Three year revenue scenario"><table class="numbers"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Metric</th><th scope="col">Year 1</th><th scope="col">Year 2</th><th scope="col">Year 3</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>New customers</td><td>8</td><td>13</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>Customers lost</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Customers at year-end</td><td>8</td><td>20</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td>Assumed billable customer-months</td><td>48</td><td>168</td><td>360</td></tr><tr><td>Implementation revenue</td><td>$200,000</td><td>$325,000</td><td>$550,000</td></tr><tr><td>Recurring revenue earned during year</td><td>$96,000</td><td>$336,000</td><td>$720,000</td></tr><tr class="total"><td>Total revenue</td><td>$296,000</td><td>$661,000</td><td>$1,270,000</td></tr><tr class="total"><td>Year-end recurring annual run rate</td><td>$192,000</td><td>$480,000</td><td>$960,000</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>This assumes implementations are completed within the relevant year. Billing dates and churn timing determine actual recurring revenue.</p>
<p>It illustrates a credible route to a million-dollar-plus service business. It does not establish a venture-scale platform.</p>
<p>Delivery economics are the deciding factor:</p>
<ul><li>At <strong>100 implementation hours × $125 loaded cost</strong>, delivery costs $12,500, leaving 50% implementation gross margin.</li><li>At <strong>200 hours</strong>, the entire $25,000 implementation fee is consumed before sales and overhead.</li><li>At <strong>six support hours/month × $100</strong>, plus $100 of attributable tools, a $2,000 monthly fee leaves 65% gross margin.</li><li>At <strong>15 support hours/month</strong>, that margin drops to 20%.</li></ul>
<p>These margins exclude sales, shared product development, administration, and other overhead. Founder time must carry a cost.</p>
<p>At 40 customers, even six support hours per customer means <strong>240 support hours per month</strong>. Twenty-two annual implementations at 100 hours each add another 2,200 hours. That already requires a team alongside sales and platform development.</p>
<p>For scale context, <strong>$10 million ARR at $24,000 per customer requires approximately 417 retained customers</strong>. Reaching that scale requires strong standardization and distribution.</p>
</section>
<section id="launch">
<h2>How I would launch it</h2>
<p>Start with one offer:</p>
<blockquote><p>“Private purchase-order intake and exception management for industrial distributors.”</p></blockquote>
<p>Use manufacturing instead if you have materially better customer access there. Founder access can outweigh an abstract market ranking.</p>
<p>For the first 90 days:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Interview 20 qualified companies in one segment.</strong> Review actual workflows, exception categories, monthly volumes, current software, and who owns the budget. Identify why existing features have not solved the problem.</li><li><strong>Sell three paid discoveries at $2,500 each</strong>, credited toward implementation. Produce a workflow map, integration assessment, privacy requirements, and measurable acceptance criteria.</li><li><strong>Secure two scoped implementations.</strong> Use milestone payments rather than funding complete custom builds yourself.</li><li><strong>Measure the reusable portion.</strong> Track engineering hours, exception-handling time, integration failures, and ongoing support effort.</li><li><strong>Continue only if the economics work.</strong> A useful initial target is measured customer payback under 12 months and a path toward 100 delivery hours or less for subsequent installations.</li></ol>
<p>Define acceptance before development: handling time, required-field correctness, exception routing, duplicate prevention, authorization, audit trails, and recovery from failed integrations. Screen approval alone is insufficient.</p>
<p>Keep human programmers and a workflow analyst visible in delivery. They can build trust and clarify responsibility. The platform can automate more of their work as patterns repeat.</p>
<div class="closing"><p><strong>My recommendation is to pursue this as a focused, productized workflow business first.</strong> Your most promising advantage is accumulating deep knowledge of one industry’s documents, exceptions, integrations, and deployment needs. If the first ten customers repeatedly buy substantially the same application, you will have concrete evidence—and reusable components—for the broader OnDemandApps.ai platform.</p></div>
</section>
</main>
